339:\subsection{The limits of online algorithms}\label{sec:impossibility-ideas}
341:In Section~\ref{sec:impossibility-proof} we prove Theorem~\ref{thm:impossibility-formal}, that there is a vanishingly small probability that any $\Alg \in \online(n)$ can produce a solution to $\MCS$ that is better than half-optimal. We show in Lemma~\ref{lem:exists-deterministic} that it suffices to consider deterministic algorithms. At a high level, we relate the probability that $\Alg$ finds a large solution to the probability that $\Alg$ finds a large solution within every input from a carefully-constructed set of correlated inputs, which will ensure that the solutions have substantial mutual overlap. Finally, we will use a combinatorial argument and the first moment method to show that such a set of solutions exists with vanishingly small probability.
